 We can use the law of total probability to find the probability that the third roll is a six. Let A be the event that the die chosen is the fair die, and B be the event that the die chosen is the biased one. Let C be the event that the first two rolls are sixes. We know that P(A) = P(B) = $\frac{1}{2}$, P(C|A) = $\left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^2$, and P(C|B) = $\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2$. Using the law of total probability, we get
\begin{align*}
P(B|C) &= \frac{P(C|B)P(B)}{P(C|B)P(B) + P(C|A)P(A)} \\
&= \frac{\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)}{\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) + \left(\frac{1}{6}\right)^2\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)} \\
&= \frac{8}{17}
\end{align*}
